// PENDING: from an OPEN SigmaHQ pull request. Not reviewed or merged by SigmaHQ yet.
// Pull request: #6111 Add rule: potential NTLM authentication coercion tool execution
// Pull request URL: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/pull/6111
// Pull request author: ashish-cybersec
// Change: new rule
// Title: Potential NTLM Authentication Coercion Tool Execution
// Sigma ID: bfda903c-f36f-432b-ae0f-fba97afc34a8
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: Ashish Pulivarthy (ashish-cybersec)
// Date: 2026-07-03 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: credential-access, T1187
// Description: Detects execution of tools that perform NTLM authentication coercion by abusing Windows RPC
//   interfaces. These tools force a target machine (typically a Domain Controller) to authenticate
//   outbound to an attacker-controlled listener using its machine account credentials, enabling NTLM
//   relay attacks and potential full domain compromise. Covered tools and techniques: - DFSCoerce:
//   abuses the MS-DFSNM (Distributed File System Namespace Management) RPC interface. -
//   ShadowCoerce: abuses the MS-FSRVP (File Server Remote VSS Protocol) RPC interface. - Coercer: a
//   meta-tool by p0dalirius that automates coercion via multiple RPC interfaces (MS-DFSNM, MS-FSRVP,
//   MS-RPRN, MS-EFSRPC, MS-ICPR, and others). - WSPCoerce: abuses the MS-WSP (Windows Search
//   Protocol) RPC interface. This rule detects the *coercion phase* (forcing authentication) and
//   complements existing rules covering the *relay phase* (ntlmrelayx, Responder, Impacket). Note:
//   DFSCoerce and PrinterBug remain unpatched on default Windows Server 2019/2022 configurations as
//   of 2026, as Microsoft does not classify forced authentication as a vulnerability when
//   credentials are required.
// False positives:
//   - Unlikely. These tool names and command patterns have no legitimate use in production environments.
// References:
//   - https://github.com/Wh04m1001/DFSCoerce
//   - https://blog.redteam-pentesting.de/2025/windows-coercion/
//   - https://github.com/p0dalirius/Coercer
//   - https://github.com/ShutdownRepo/ShadowCoerce
//   - https://github.com/RedTeamPentesting/wspcoerce
//   - https://lyrie.ai/research/research/ntlm-relay-2026-defensive-playbook
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/68d50561ed22c52e05ea3bda490edd37755a10ab/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_hktl_ntlm_coercion_tools.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath contains "DFSCoerce" or FolderPath contains "dfscoerce" or FolderPath contains "ShadowCoerce" or FolderPath contains "shadowcoerce" or FolderPath contains "WSPCoerce" or FolderPath contains "wspcoerce") or (ProcessCommandLine contains "DFSCoerce.py" or ProcessCommandLine contains "dfscoerce.py" or ProcessCommandLine contains "ShadowCoerce.py" or ProcessCommandLine contains "shadowcoerce.py" or ProcessCommandLine contains "WSPCoerce.py" or ProcessCommandLine contains "wspcoerce.py") or (FolderPath contains "coercer" and (ProcessCommandLine contains "coercer coerce" or ProcessCommandLine contains "coercer scan" or ProcessCommandLine contains "coercer fuzz" or ProcessCommandLine contains "-m coerce" or ProcessCommandLine contains "from coercer"))
